From 81fa5e498fd7ce28ca6cb6b1e06635580e092a55 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Lamb <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 21:23:59 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 4/7] offers: serve invoice requests and pay offers over onion
 messages

The node now answers invoice requests for its offers, which is how a
mining pool that pays to offers pays a miner, and fetches and pays other
nodes' offers.

Serving (offerserve): a delivered invoice_request is checked as a reader
on this chain, signature included; the offer it mirrors must verify as
minted by this node, must have arrived over one of the offer's blinded
paths when it has them and not over a blinded path when it has none, and
must be enabled and unexpired. A record lost to a restore from seed is
stored again from the request, since the offer's metadata proves it is
ours. An invoice with blinded payment paths is created in the registry,
wrapped as a BOLT 12 invoice, signed with the node key through the
keychain's tagged Schnorr signer, checked the way a payer will check it,
recorded, and sent over the requester's reply path. Refusals go back as
invoice_error with a fixed text; requests for offers that are not ours,
that came the wrong way, or that carry no reply path are ignored before
any work. A repeated request gets its earlier invoice while that lasts.
Requests are limited per peer and overall, and records of invoices that
expired unpaid are pruned.

Paying (offerpay): a signed request with a fresh payer key goes over one
of the offer's paths, or to the issuer's node id when the offer has none,
with a reply path whose path_id ties the reply to the fetch. The invoice
is checked against the request, including that invoice_node_id is the
issuer's key or the path's final blinded node. The invoice's paths become
the router's blinded path set. When a path starts at this node, because
the node is the issuer's channel peer, the router would refuse it, so our
own hop is processed here: its data decrypted, the next node taken from
the channel it names, the next blinding point derived, and the path's
aggregate fee and expiry reduced by our relay policy the way our hop
would apply them when forwarding, with BOLT 4's rounding.

The offers sub-server gains FetchInvoice, PayOffer and ListOfferInvoices,
and lncli offer fetchinvoice, pay and invoices. A failed payment reports
the payment hash and the invoice to retry with, since a failed attempt
may still settle and fetching again would make a second invoice; paying a
settled invoice again returns its preimage. The fee limit defaults as for
payinvoice. Without onion messages, fetching and paying are refused.

The offer id is now Core Lightning's, the SHA256 of the offer's TLV
bytes, pinned by a test against an offer an unmodified lightningd
decoded; BOLT 12 defines none, and this way the two tools agree. Offer
paths are built under a session key derived from the offer, so minting
the same offer again through the same peers gives the same offer.

docs/bolt12-offers.md tells an operator how to be paid by a pool, what
survives a restore, and how to pay and retry.

